A||N||2||kornholio||filling up cooling system||||5
Z||000000||kornholio||10-11-1999||10:24 AM||||My cooling system is very low and I want to add coolant to the radiator. Is it the same procedure to "burp" it as if I were draining the whole system then refilling it? The Haynes manual says to: 1)pull out the thermostat, 2)fill the rad, 3)run to operating temp, 3)fill the rad again, replace thermostat and caps, 4)fill the recovery tank. Does this procedure apply to me? Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by kornholio (edited 10-11-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||David||10-11-1999||12:42 PM||||You should follow these instrutions, for the most part.  Remove the theromstat and the radiator cap.  Fill the system at the theromstat neck and watch for water to come out of the radiator.  At this point I put everything back together, overfill the overflow tank a little bit, and then drive the car until it gets to normal operating temperature.  After it has then cooled, I then remove the thermostat and radiator cap to insure that they are both full.  At this point I have never had to add any fluid.  Note: this is for my 4 cyl. <p>[This message has been edited by David (edited 10-11-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||10-12-1999||05:07 AM||||It also helps to park the car on a slight<BR>downhill, with the front of the fiero pointing<BR>down the hill. When the fluids start to<BR>come out of the radiator, replace the cap<BR>and continue to add to the thermostat<BR>housing, until full. <P>Ross||||reg||1
